* @title Gimpel chapter 11 implementation and timing helpers
* @attribution James F. Gimpel, Algorithms in SNOBOL4, Chapter 11; Catspaw SNOBOL4+ program diskette
* @expect >>>
* SYSTEM = IBM MAINBOL
* RESOLUTION POSITIVE = YES
* LPROG_A = 81
* LPROG_B = 82
* LPROG_C = 83
* <<<
* The deterministic helpers from Chapter 11 are exercised end-to-
* end: SYSTEM() identifies the processor by &ALPHABET size and an
* OPSYN-driven precedence probe, RESOLUTION() spins until TIME()
* ticks, and LPROG() returns &STNO of a freshly compiled probe
* statement (so each call increments).
*
* TIMER and TIMEGC are -INCLUDEd to verify they parse and define
* without error, but they are not invoked: their reported output
* is the measured millisecond cost of a statement or a typical
* garbage collect, which is non-deterministic.
*
* FPROFILE and TPROFILE are omitted entirely. Both install
* TRACE(.STCOUNT,'KEYWORD',...) on the statement-execution
* counter, which neither snoflake nor stock CSNOBOL4 fire on the
* executing program, so the profiler arrays never accumulate.
-INCLUDE "LPROG.INC"
-INCLUDE "RESOLUTI.INC"
-INCLUDE "SYSTEM.INC"
-INCLUDE "TIMER.INC"
-INCLUDE "TIMEGC.INC"
	OUTPUT = 'SYSTEM = ' SYSTEM()
	OUTPUT = 'RESOLUTION POSITIVE = ' (GT(RESOLUTION(),0) 'YES')
	OUTPUT = 'LPROG_A = ' LPROG()
	OUTPUT = 'LPROG_B = ' LPROG()
	OUTPUT = 'LPROG_C = ' LPROG()
END
